## 4. A 5-point availability improvement can resemble another scanner

Use a simplified fleet of 20 scanners, each capable of 230 wafers per hour when running.

Nominal throughput is:

20 × 230 = 4,600 wafers per hour.

At 90% availability, effective available throughput is:

4,600 × 90% = 4,140 wafers per hour.

At 95% availability:

4,600 × 95% = 4,370 wafers per hour.

The difference is **230 wafers per hour** — equal to the nominal throughput of one additional scanner in this simplified example.

Do not interpret 90% or 95% as a claim about a specific customer’s current EUV fleet. They are teaching assumptions.

The point is structural:

**on a large installed base, a few percentage points of availability can have the capacity effect of expensive new hardware.**

## 5. Why service becomes economically important

This creates a second ASML business around the installed fleet.

ASML calls its service and field-option revenue **Installed Base Management**. In 2025, that category generated about €8.2 billion versus €32.7 billion of total ASML sales — roughly one-quarter of company revenue.

That figure is not pure repair revenue; it also includes field options and upgrades. But it shows why the economics do not end when a scanner is shipped.

Once a fab has billions of euros of ASML equipment embedded in a qualified production flow, it needs continuing parts, service, software, upgrades and engineering support to protect output.

This is recurring revenue attached to an installed capital base.

## 6. Why the service moat can be strong

A fab cannot treat an EUV scanner like generic factory equipment and call any local repair shop.

The tool is deeply integrated with ASML hardware, software, diagnostics, spare-parts logistics and process knowledge. A poorly executed repair or calibration can reduce throughput, overlay performance or yield even if the machine technically turns back on.

The switching problem is therefore not just contractual.

It is operational risk.

For a leading-edge fab, the cost of losing production on an EUV bottleneck can be far larger than the price of the service intervention itself.

That gives the original equipment vendor unusually strong economic relevance over the life of the tool.

